## Why This Is Asked
Assess hands-on orchestration across Azure components during incidents, focusing on tenant isolation, automated rollback safety, and auditable controls.

## Key Concepts
- UEBA-driven automation (Defender for Cloud/Sentinel)
- Per-tenant network isolation (NSG, Private Endpoint)
- Key Vault key rotation and re-encryption
- Token revocation and Conditional Access control
- Canary-based rollback mechanisms
- Centralized auditing and alerting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator snippet
async function quarantineTenant(tenantId){
  await applyPerTenantNSG(tenantId);
  await rotateKeyVaultKeys(tenantId);
  await revokeTokensAndDisableCA(tenantId);
  await rollbackToLastGoodRelease(tenantId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates ability to implement scalable, workload-driven security without NSG churn, leveraging Azure Firewall Manager, policy-based groups, Private Endpoints, and identity-based controls.

## Key Concepts
- Workload tagging and policy-based groups for dynamic segmentation
- Centralized firewall policies vs. per-VM NSGs
- Private Endpoints for service-to-service traffic
- Managed Identities for authenticating inter-service calls
- Azure Policy to enforce tagging across subscriptions
- Observability via Defender for Cloud and Log Analytics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Azure Policy snippet to require workload-id tag on resources
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        { "field": "type", "in": [
          "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks",
          "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets"
        ]},
        { "field": "tags['workload-id']", "exists": "false" }
      ]
    },
    "then": { "effect": "deny" }
  },
  "name": "require-workload-id-tag",
  "properties": {
    "displayName": "Require workload-id tag on network scope",
    "policyType": "BuiltIn"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback and drift if a workload-id tag is accidentally removed?
- What metrics would you surface to confirm zero-trust posture without degrading performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess storage security, policy enforcement, and rollback capabilities in a practical scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Policy deny public network access
- Private Endpoints and VNET integration
- Managed Identities RBAC
- Secrets in Key Vault instead of config

## Code Example
```json
{
  "mode": "Indexed",
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        {"field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts"},
        {"field": "properties.publicNetworkAccess", "equals": "Enabled"}
      ]
    },
    "then": {"effect": "deny"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate rollback after applying policy?
- How would you handle temporary public access needs during migrations?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes real-world guardrail design for ML deployments in a multi-tenant Azure environment, focusing on data isolation, secret management, and trustworthy rollout processes.

## Key Concepts

- Azure Lighthouse and cross-tenant delegation
- Row-Level Security and Dynamic Data Masking
- Canary deployments and automatic rollback
- Azure Policy to enforce Key Vault references
- Data provenance and model-output leakage mitigation
- UEBA monitoring and cross-tenant audit trails

## Code Example

```json
{
  \"policyRule\": {
    \"if\": {
      \"anyOf\": [
        {\"field\": \"type\", \"equals\": \"Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults/secrets\"},
        {\"field\": \"siteConfig.appSettings[0]\", \"exists\": true}
      ]
    },
    \"then\": {\"effect\": \"deny\"}
  }
